503-
504-## Import Auth from Your Browser
505-
506:The fastest way to authenticate is to reuse cookies from a Chrome session you are already logged into.
507-
508-**Step 1: Start Chrome with remote debugging**
509-
--
535-# Load auth at launch
536-agent-browser --state ./my-auth.json open https://app.example.com/dashboard
537-
538:# Or load into an existing session
539-agent-browser state load ./my-auth.json
540-agent-browser open https://app.example.com/dashboard
541-```
542-
543:This works for any site, including those with complex OAuth flows, SSO, or 2FA -- as long as Chrome already has valid session cookies.
544-
545:> **Security note:** State files contain session tokens in plaintext. Add them to `.gitignore`, delete when no longer needed, and set `AGENT_BROWSER_ENCRYPTION_KEY` for encryption at rest. See [Security Best Practices](#security-best-practices).
546-
547:**Tip:** Combine with `--session-name` so the imported auth auto-persists across restarts:
548-
549-```bash
550:agent-browser --session-name myapp state load ./my-auth.json
551-# From now on, state is auto-saved/restored for "myapp"
552-```
553-
--
574-Or set via environment variable:
575-
576-```bash
577:export AGENT_BROWSER_PROFILE=~/.myapp-profile
578-agent-browser open https://app.example.com/dashboard
579-```
580-
581-## Session Persistence
582-
583:Use `--session-name` to auto-save and restore cookies + localStorage by name, without managing files:
584-
585-```bash
586-# Auto-saves state on close, auto-restores on next launch
587:agent-browser --session-name twitter open https://twitter.com
588-# ... login flow ...
589:agent-browser close  # state saved to ~/.agent-browser/sessions/
590-
591-# Next time: state is automatically restored
592:agent-browser --session-name twitter open https://twitter.com
593-```
594-
595-Encrypt state at rest:
596-
597-```bash
598:export AGENT_BROWSER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
599:agent-browser --session-name secure open https://app.example.com
600-```
601-
